WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.144
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.144 --> 00:00:04.310
PAUL NASH: So I'm Paul Nash.

00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:06.680
I'm the product lead
for Compute Engine.

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:08.930
And I have with me
today Michael Crandell,

00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:10.460
CEO and founder of Rightscale.

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:12.140
He'll be up in a little bit.

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:17.060
So today we wanted to talk
about Google's approach

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:21.310
to cloud pricing in general, and
how we think about customers,

00:00:21.310 --> 00:00:25.220
and what we try to optimize
for in building products.

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:27.830
And then, specifically
for Compute Engine,

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:29.630
we want to talk
about the customer

00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:31.250
friendly pricing
features that we've

00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:33.860
come up with that we think
are really a great way

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:35.870
to use the cloud.

00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:37.940
Then we're going to talk
a little bit about how,

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:39.857
beyond that set of
features and discounts,

00:00:39.857 --> 00:00:42.440
there are some other things you
can do to add to your workload

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:43.935
to save even more money.

00:00:43.935 --> 00:00:45.560
And then finally,
we'll have Michael up

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:49.940
to show some real world examples
using customer data of what

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:52.379
that looks like on paper.

00:00:52.379 --> 00:00:54.170
And then we'll take
your questions as well,

00:00:54.170 --> 00:00:57.320
or you can all run
away and have beer.

00:00:57.320 --> 00:00:59.420
I'm fine either way.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:03.770
So Google's approach to cloud
starts with Google's approach

00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:06.440
to customers, in general.

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:12.050
And one of the things that
I mean about that is we

00:01:12.050 --> 00:01:13.850
have a set of core
values at Google

00:01:13.850 --> 00:01:18.620
about key principles like data
security, privacy and above all

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:20.300
user trust.

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:23.240
So we believe that maintaining
our users and customers'

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:27.200
trust in Google is looking
out for their best interests

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:29.664
as one of our highest callings.

00:01:29.664 --> 00:01:31.580
So additionally, our
goal is to build products

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:34.096
that are simple to
use and well-designed,

00:01:34.096 --> 00:01:35.720
and this includes
how you pay for them.

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:43.910
So what that boils down to
in Compute Engine is really

00:01:43.910 --> 00:01:47.450
three core pricing
philosophy principles that we

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:49.640
use to think about
how we're pricing

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:51.540
and how were building discounts.

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:54.530
The first is it
should be simple.

00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:56.090
So no rocket science.

00:01:56.090 --> 00:01:57.950
No PhDs in economics.

00:01:57.950 --> 00:02:00.890
No ministries of pricing
within your companies as Urs

00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:02.370
said this morning.

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:04.550
And no hidden costs.

00:02:04.550 --> 00:02:07.040
Second, it should be flexible.

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:10.759
And what that means is
you pay for what you use.

00:02:10.759 --> 00:02:13.970
Apparently, the whole point of
cloud is to be elastic, right?

00:02:13.970 --> 00:02:16.850
Well, somethings are
more elastic than others.

00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:19.610
And so for that to
be true the pricing

00:02:19.610 --> 00:02:23.360
can't rope the developers
into a certain shape

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:26.630
of what you're using, or how
long you have to use it for,

00:02:26.630 --> 00:02:28.580
or what you can do with it.

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:31.070
And finally, then, we
think the real embodiment

00:02:31.070 --> 00:02:34.100
of user customer
trust is the idea

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:35.720
that prices should be fair.

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:39.560
So we should start with making
prices low, as low as we can.

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:43.610
And then, if we find ways
to bring additional savings

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:46.490
into the ecosystem, either
because we're scaling

00:02:46.490 --> 00:02:48.200
or because we got
a better hardware,

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:50.270
or we found some new
efficiencies in how

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:52.880
our software is
managing that hardware,

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:56.300
you should get those savings
passed on to you as well.

00:02:56.300 --> 00:02:59.790
And that includes also the
concept of automatic discounts.

00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:03.765
So if you use the cloud in a way
that is more efficient for us,

00:03:03.765 --> 00:03:05.390
we should pass those
savings on to you.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:11.080
So what do I mean
when I say simplicity?

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:16.280
Well, this is a graphic that
one of our product managers

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:17.680
came up with.

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:20.390
It's a little
faded out, but this

00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:24.680
is a screen capture
of our pricing page

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:28.350
sort of laid sideways, and one
of the other clouds pricing

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:31.910
pages laid sideways.

00:03:31.910 --> 00:03:34.280
So it's a pretty
obvious representation

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:38.120
of how much do you have
to ingest and understand

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:42.530
in order to figure out what's
going on, how much am I paying.

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:44.210
I spend a lot of time
with these pages.

00:03:44.210 --> 00:03:45.955
I'm sure some of
you have as well.

00:03:45.955 --> 00:03:47.930
They are lovely pages.

00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:49.430
But what I find is
that I also spend

00:03:49.430 --> 00:03:53.240
a lot of time with Chrome's
search in page feature

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:56.000
to find where the
information is on that page

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:58.970
that I'm trying to understand
how much is a customer being

00:03:58.970 --> 00:04:00.320
charged for that.

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:05.970
And it's not at all obvious that
that is a good use of my time.

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:07.840
In fact, usually I
don't consider it is.

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:10.100
I usually end up building
spreadsheets, in fact,

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:13.820
to break that information apart
and try to understand it more.

00:04:13.820 --> 00:04:16.339
Wouldn't just be simpler if
you didn't have to do that,

00:04:16.339 --> 00:04:17.936
and you can focus
on your application?

00:04:17.936 --> 00:04:19.060
That's what we think about.

00:04:21.860 --> 00:04:25.550
So one of the main ways that
pricing can get complicated

00:04:25.550 --> 00:04:29.420
is in how various [? IS ?]
resources are bundled together

00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:31.220
by some of the providers.

00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:33.770
So this creates dozens
of different pre-canned

00:04:33.770 --> 00:04:34.760
configurations--

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:37.220
I call it alphabet soup--

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:39.830
which are essentially
optimized for different classes

00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:41.030
of workloads.

00:04:41.030 --> 00:04:43.880
And the problem is,
as always, that that

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:45.500
requires a bunch
of generalizations

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:47.240
and a bunch of assumptions.

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:49.070
What if you need
all the RAM, but you

00:04:49.070 --> 00:04:50.930
don't need all of the CPU?

00:04:50.930 --> 00:04:55.340
Or what about that SSD that
you got for free in that VM?

00:04:55.340 --> 00:04:56.430
Are you using that?

00:04:56.430 --> 00:04:58.440
Because it's not free.

00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:00.710
So understanding the
basic combinations

00:05:00.710 --> 00:05:01.610
and all the sizes--

00:05:01.610 --> 00:05:03.920
that can be kind
of a full time job.

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:06.980
And trying to figure out,
for example, simple things,

00:05:06.980 --> 00:05:09.560
like how much are you
paying for one of the CPUs

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:12.380
in your machine, or
for a gigabyte of RAM,

00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:13.760
it's hard to tell for sure.

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:16.106
And it's not always the same.

00:05:16.106 --> 00:05:17.480
And what's worse,
every time they

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:19.280
add a new generation
of hardware,

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:21.410
you get a whole new set
of skews to figure out.

00:05:21.410 --> 00:05:23.180
This is a problem we
talk about internally

00:05:23.180 --> 00:05:24.870
called skew explosion.

00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:28.220
So you have some giant page of
all the different price points

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:29.510
and how much everything costs.

00:05:29.510 --> 00:05:32.510
And every time there's a new
discount model, or a new tweak,

00:05:32.510 --> 00:05:34.790
or a new feature, you
get a whole another set

00:05:34.790 --> 00:05:35.759
of those skews.

00:05:35.759 --> 00:05:36.800
It's difficult to manage.

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:38.510
It's difficult to understand.

00:05:38.510 --> 00:05:40.880
And so we try to
find a better way.

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:46.040
So by comparison, we have one
main family that's called N1.

00:05:46.040 --> 00:05:49.160
There's a couple very special
machine types called F1 and G1.

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:52.100
But almost all of our usage,
and almost all of our customers

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:53.540
are using N1.

00:05:53.540 --> 00:05:55.640
We might someday
have a second family,

00:05:55.640 --> 00:05:57.770
but it's kind of a
big decision for us.

00:05:57.770 --> 00:06:00.260
So we just have the one for now.

00:06:00.260 --> 00:06:03.290
And what we try to do without is
give you a transparent pricing

00:06:03.290 --> 00:06:03.980
performance.

00:06:03.980 --> 00:06:07.580
So that allows us
to have as skews

00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:10.250
as we possibly can to
understand in the first place.

00:06:10.250 --> 00:06:12.860
The fewer things to
understand, the better.

00:06:12.860 --> 00:06:14.870
And with something like
custom machine types

00:06:14.870 --> 00:06:16.470
is actually only two.

00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:21.440
There's how much a CPU costs,
and how much does RAM cost.

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:25.630
So that lets you buy
just what you need.

00:06:25.630 --> 00:06:27.270
And you can use it
however you want,

00:06:27.270 --> 00:06:29.234
either is one of our
predefined chips,

00:06:29.234 --> 00:06:30.400
or as a custom machine type.

00:06:35.850 --> 00:06:38.930
So with a custom machine types
and the predefined chips,

00:06:38.930 --> 00:06:41.780
we put some predefineds
in there just so

00:06:41.780 --> 00:06:44.280
that it would be a little
bit easier to get started.

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:46.955
So there's a couple
typical ratios of RAM

00:06:46.955 --> 00:06:50.000
to CPU that a lot of
customers will find adequate

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:51.320
for most of their workloads.

00:06:51.320 --> 00:06:54.770
But you should just think of
those little marks on the dials

00:06:54.770 --> 00:06:56.894
that you can use to start.

00:06:56.894 --> 00:06:58.560
If you want to start
there that's great.

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:01.160
If you don't, you can build
a custom machine type.

00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:03.530
And either way if you
find later that you're not

00:07:03.530 --> 00:07:05.420
using the right
amount of resources,

00:07:05.420 --> 00:07:07.670
you can optimize that easily.

00:07:07.670 --> 00:07:09.680
In fact we will
help you do that.

00:07:12.710 --> 00:07:18.130
So a VM at its
essence, of course,

00:07:18.130 --> 00:07:20.320
is just that CPU and RAM.

00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:23.680
And anything else we
view is an add-on.

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:25.900
So another way that
we try to keep things

00:07:25.900 --> 00:07:28.690
simple and transparent
is by trying

00:07:28.690 --> 00:07:30.760
to not bundle in
the different pieces

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:34.300
together, and giving you
an opaque product that

00:07:34.300 --> 00:07:37.300
hides the different costs
of those various components.

00:07:37.300 --> 00:07:40.490
So why do the others
do it this way?

00:07:40.490 --> 00:07:42.700
Well, it's easier.

00:07:42.700 --> 00:07:45.910
But it's easier for
them, not for you.

00:07:45.910 --> 00:07:50.230
So pre-configuring a hardware
according to a fixed recipe,

00:07:50.230 --> 00:07:53.530
that would sure make it easier
for us to manage our fleet,

00:07:53.530 --> 00:07:57.590
but it would make you
inherit the complexity.

00:07:57.590 --> 00:07:59.780
So what would end
up happening is

00:07:59.780 --> 00:08:02.470
that you'd pay the price
because you pay for things

00:08:02.470 --> 00:08:03.160
you don't need.

00:08:05.814 --> 00:08:07.230
And they'd all be
bundled together

00:08:07.230 --> 00:08:09.120
in whatever alphabet
letter you decided

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:11.460
was the right VM
for your workload.

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:14.040
So we decided to keep
our prices visible

00:08:14.040 --> 00:08:15.600
and let you choose
what you need.

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:17.599
And then we'll take care
of the fleet efficiency

00:08:17.599 --> 00:08:20.470
behind the scenes
using Google magic.

00:08:20.470 --> 00:08:22.950
So just to give you an example
of one of the things that

00:08:22.950 --> 00:08:26.880
tends to get bundled in on
some clouds is how much does it

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:30.330
cost to run Windows on a VM?

00:08:30.330 --> 00:08:32.880
Well, turns out you
can't really tell,

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:35.039
unless you go back
to that long page

00:08:35.039 --> 00:08:37.260
and build a spreadsheet
and crack everything apart.

00:08:37.260 --> 00:08:38.801
And once you do
that, what you'll see

00:08:38.801 --> 00:08:40.289
is that, depending
on the alphabet

00:08:40.289 --> 00:08:43.770
letter that you choose, the
cost of Windows per core

00:08:43.770 --> 00:08:45.950
is different.

00:08:45.950 --> 00:08:48.710
But that's kind of strange
because the costs for us

00:08:48.710 --> 00:08:51.110
to sell you a Windows
license per core

00:08:51.110 --> 00:08:54.850
doesn't change if we run it
on a faster or slower CPU,

00:08:54.850 --> 00:08:59.300
or on one that has more RAM, or
memory, or disk attached to it.

00:08:59.300 --> 00:09:02.600
So on Google Cloud,
it's always $0.04

00:09:02.600 --> 00:09:04.640
per core per hour,
no matter what.

00:09:08.550 --> 00:09:12.870
So now I want to talk
about flexibility.

00:09:12.870 --> 00:09:14.800
And that really means
these two concepts.

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:18.870
So pay for what you use
and buy only what you need.

00:09:18.870 --> 00:09:20.650
Sounds simple, right?

00:09:20.650 --> 00:09:22.520
Everybody does it?

00:09:22.520 --> 00:09:25.570
Not so much.

00:09:25.570 --> 00:09:29.050
So everybody assumes that
cloud is pay as you use.

00:09:29.050 --> 00:09:31.810
But it turns out that the
way that you count usage

00:09:31.810 --> 00:09:33.890
matters a lot in practice.

00:09:33.890 --> 00:09:37.390
So in GCU, we've had
permanent billing from day one

00:09:37.390 --> 00:09:39.370
because at Google we
understand that workloads

00:09:39.370 --> 00:09:43.160
don't come in our long
chunks most of the time.

00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:45.160
So for example,
imagine that you had

00:09:45.160 --> 00:09:48.610
a set of VMs illustrated
just below by this bar chart

00:09:48.610 --> 00:09:52.939
that we're running for
different periods of time.

00:09:52.939 --> 00:09:54.980
And there are a number of
ways this could happen.

00:09:54.980 --> 00:09:57.640
One is that maybe they're part
of an auto scaled workload.

00:09:57.640 --> 00:10:00.231
And some of them are only needed
for a small amount of time.

00:10:00.231 --> 00:10:02.230
And some of them are
needed for most of the time

00:10:02.230 --> 00:10:04.680
that you're
experiencing a spike.

00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:06.430
Another way that
this may happen is

00:10:06.430 --> 00:10:08.020
if you're doing some
batch processing

00:10:08.020 --> 00:10:09.895
and there's a number of
different tasks being

00:10:09.895 --> 00:10:12.790
distributed to VMs, then
you need some for a while,

00:10:12.790 --> 00:10:15.130
and then others you
don't need for a while.

00:10:15.130 --> 00:10:17.295
So moving rendering
might be a good example

00:10:17.295 --> 00:10:18.670
of that where you
don't know what

00:10:18.670 --> 00:10:20.440
the frame time is going to be.

00:10:20.440 --> 00:10:25.360
Well, if on the left
you had 0 to 60 minutes,

00:10:25.360 --> 00:10:27.910
and your workload takes
the amount of time in blue,

00:10:27.910 --> 00:10:30.430
then all that stuff
in there is wasted.

00:10:30.430 --> 00:10:32.650
And in this particular
hypothetical example,

00:10:32.650 --> 00:10:34.870
that's like 40% of
what you're paying.

00:10:39.130 --> 00:10:41.315
So this is a slightly
different scenario

00:10:41.315 --> 00:10:43.690
that we model to illustrate
what would happen if you were

00:10:43.690 --> 00:10:45.430
using an auto scaled workload.

00:10:45.430 --> 00:10:48.010
So in this example,
the bar charts

00:10:48.010 --> 00:10:50.080
are representing a
set of VMs you have

00:10:50.080 --> 00:10:53.830
where every VM can run
20 queries per second.

00:10:53.830 --> 00:10:57.760
And let's imagine that
this is a website,

00:10:57.760 --> 00:11:00.940
and a talk show
host-- famous talk

00:11:00.940 --> 00:11:04.000
show host-- has just mentioned
our website on live TV.

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:05.800
And we had this burst.

00:11:05.800 --> 00:11:09.040
And everybody wants to
come and buy our widget.

00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:11.980
So in order to
react to that scale

00:11:11.980 --> 00:11:14.590
you're going to spin up
something like 50 VMs.

00:11:14.590 --> 00:11:16.090
But here's the thing--

00:11:16.090 --> 00:11:19.510
when you go to spin those
down, whether you spin them

00:11:19.510 --> 00:11:22.300
down right away or not,
if you're paying per hour,

00:11:22.300 --> 00:11:25.720
you're still paying for another
chunk of usage that's almost as

00:11:25.720 --> 00:11:26.890
big as a spike itself.

00:11:26.890 --> 00:11:29.290
In fact, it looks a
little bit bigger.

00:11:29.290 --> 00:11:32.440
If you pay per minute, it's
scaled down much faster

00:11:32.440 --> 00:11:34.630
as the traffic dies away.

00:11:34.630 --> 00:11:37.510
And this graph doesn't have
to be blocky like it is here.

00:11:37.510 --> 00:11:39.430
This is just shown at
five minute intervals

00:11:39.430 --> 00:11:42.100
to give you an idea of
what the steps look like.

00:11:42.100 --> 00:11:44.940
But in reality, our
auto scaler at least

00:11:44.940 --> 00:11:46.430
resamples every 60 seconds.

00:11:46.430 --> 00:11:50.110
So it would follow that
curve much more smoothly.

00:11:53.510 --> 00:11:56.870
So finally, another
use case we see a lot--

00:11:56.870 --> 00:11:59.000
so imagine that you
have a workload that

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:02.480
takes about four hours to run,
and it runs on a four core

00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:03.960
machine currently.

00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:06.620
Now, let's say you go and do
a bunch of optimization work.

00:12:06.620 --> 00:12:08.120
And you've got it
to the point where

00:12:08.120 --> 00:12:09.920
you can scale up that workload.

00:12:09.920 --> 00:12:13.220
And you're getting about
a 1.8x scaling factor--

00:12:13.220 --> 00:12:15.680
could be 100, but
it's not every 2.0.

00:12:15.680 --> 00:12:17.770
If you wanted double
the number of cores,

00:12:17.770 --> 00:12:20.210
you're not going to
usually get exact scaling.

00:12:20.210 --> 00:12:22.820
So let's say that you get 1.8x.

00:12:22.820 --> 00:12:26.870
So the graph on the left shows
what the run times would change

00:12:26.870 --> 00:12:28.610
and how they would
change if you went

00:12:28.610 --> 00:12:31.490
to 8 core, 16 cores,
or 32 core machine

00:12:31.490 --> 00:12:32.990
under those assumptions.

00:12:32.990 --> 00:12:34.550
And the graph on
the right shows what

00:12:34.550 --> 00:12:38.520
you would pay if you were paying
per minute versus per hour.

00:12:38.520 --> 00:12:41.600
So what happens is
the workload runtime

00:12:41.600 --> 00:12:43.970
might fall below one
of the hour marks,

00:12:43.970 --> 00:12:47.330
but you're still paying
for the extra waste.

00:12:47.330 --> 00:12:50.000
And so all of your
work-- you might

00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:53.060
have gotten your answer faster,
but you're paying more for it

00:12:53.060 --> 00:12:54.740
too.

00:12:54.740 --> 00:12:56.240
So this is one of
the key principles

00:12:56.240 --> 00:12:58.700
that we believe is important
in a truly elastic cloud.

00:13:01.560 --> 00:13:04.500
So next I want to
talk about VM sizes.

00:13:04.500 --> 00:13:09.712
So why is it that machines are
sized in powers of two anyway?

00:13:09.712 --> 00:13:10.970
Any of you know?

00:13:10.970 --> 00:13:12.490
Have an idea?

00:13:12.490 --> 00:13:13.570
I'll tell you why.

00:13:13.570 --> 00:13:14.950
It's easier.

00:13:14.950 --> 00:13:18.940
Again, though, it's easier for
whoever's building the cloud,

00:13:18.940 --> 00:13:20.980
not for you, the customer.

00:13:20.980 --> 00:13:23.990
So the computer scientists in
the room might recognize this.

00:13:23.990 --> 00:13:26.050
It's called a bin
packing problem.

00:13:26.050 --> 00:13:28.060
And if you don't
have powers of two,

00:13:28.060 --> 00:13:30.010
the concern is that
it's harder to make sure

00:13:30.010 --> 00:13:32.170
that all the
processors on a machine

00:13:32.170 --> 00:13:35.200
are filled and then used
all the time without leaving

00:13:35.200 --> 00:13:36.670
some behind.

00:13:36.670 --> 00:13:40.960
Well, it turns out at Google we
solved that problem a long time

00:13:40.960 --> 00:13:45.220
ago because we built our
cloud 10 to 15 years ago,

00:13:45.220 --> 00:13:48.280
and we have lots
of workloads too,

00:13:48.280 --> 00:13:51.170
but we need to pack them all
inefficiently on processors.

00:13:51.170 --> 00:13:54.740
And so we built a system
called Borg that does that.

00:13:54.740 --> 00:13:56.800
It further turns
out that when we

00:13:56.800 --> 00:13:58.720
went to go build
our public cloud,

00:13:58.720 --> 00:14:01.270
we built it all on that
same Borg technology.

00:14:01.270 --> 00:14:04.070
So underneath Google
Compute Engine

00:14:04.070 --> 00:14:09.010
is years of work from hundreds
of engineers figuring out

00:14:09.010 --> 00:14:12.580
how to solve that problem
without making you just pay

00:14:12.580 --> 00:14:14.140
for the inefficiency.

00:14:14.140 --> 00:14:16.510
So if I sell you power
of two size machines,

00:14:16.510 --> 00:14:20.500
like a 64 core machine, but you
actually only need 40 cores,

00:14:20.500 --> 00:14:23.159
the other 24 cores are wasted.

00:14:23.159 --> 00:14:24.700
If I'm the cloud
provider doing that,

00:14:24.700 --> 00:14:26.074
I might not be
concerned about it

00:14:26.074 --> 00:14:27.850
because you're paying for it.

00:14:27.850 --> 00:14:30.490
If I'm Google and I'm building
that cloud for myself,

00:14:30.490 --> 00:14:33.700
I'm concerned about it a lot
because I'm paying for it.

00:14:33.700 --> 00:14:35.650
And it's a massive
amount of inefficiency.

00:14:35.650 --> 00:14:38.380
So we get rid of all
that by offering you

00:14:38.380 --> 00:14:40.000
custom machine types
which are built

00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:41.703
on that same infrastructure.

00:14:45.020 --> 00:14:46.940
Another advantage that
we've seen from this

00:14:46.940 --> 00:14:50.030
is that a lot of
customers over time

00:14:50.030 --> 00:14:53.300
have purchased specific
sized hardware.

00:14:53.300 --> 00:14:57.290
And in an on premise fleet
it's common to see something

00:14:57.290 --> 00:15:00.890
like a 12 core machine that's
actually 12 physical cores.

00:15:00.890 --> 00:15:03.410
So if they want to move
that to the cloud today,

00:15:03.410 --> 00:15:07.040
they'd have to buy a 16
core VM in a lot of places.

00:15:07.040 --> 00:15:09.560
And that automatically
shifts the bill

00:15:09.560 --> 00:15:12.530
and makes the comparison
of costs difficult.

00:15:12.530 --> 00:15:15.500
So if you have something
like custom machine types,

00:15:15.500 --> 00:15:18.290
it's fine for a customer to
bring their building materials,

00:15:18.290 --> 00:15:21.320
their whole list of all the
different VM sizes and shapes

00:15:21.320 --> 00:15:23.004
that they have on prem today.

00:15:23.004 --> 00:15:24.920
If they're thinking about
lifting and shifting

00:15:24.920 --> 00:15:27.830
a workload, you can simply
configure a custom machine

00:15:27.830 --> 00:15:31.970
type that matches those sizes,
bring all your workload in,

00:15:31.970 --> 00:15:34.669
and then optimize when
it's convenient for you.

00:15:34.669 --> 00:15:36.710
So we see customers do
that a lot to get a better

00:15:36.710 --> 00:15:39.350
match between what they're
going to use in cloud,

00:15:39.350 --> 00:15:41.649
and what they're using
today on premise.

00:15:41.649 --> 00:15:43.190
In fact, we launched
this feature out

00:15:43.190 --> 00:15:45.030
just a little over a year ago.

00:15:45.030 --> 00:15:47.360
And in that time it's
grown to be about 20%

00:15:47.360 --> 00:15:50.450
of our total usage by
core hours and in GCE.

00:15:50.450 --> 00:15:52.070
And we went and
calculated the savings

00:15:52.070 --> 00:15:53.486
from what customers
would have had

00:15:53.486 --> 00:15:56.540
to spend if they were using
the next up standard size,

00:15:56.540 --> 00:15:58.100
and it turns out
those customers that

00:15:58.100 --> 00:16:00.830
are using it are saving
about 19% on average

00:16:00.830 --> 00:16:02.380
simply by not overbuying.

00:16:07.235 --> 00:16:08.985
So finally, I want to
talk about fairness.

00:16:12.364 --> 00:16:13.780
So one of the
things we talk a lot

00:16:13.780 --> 00:16:17.890
about with discounts is the
number, what's the percentage?

00:16:17.890 --> 00:16:20.230
And there's a bunch of
numbers flying around.

00:16:20.230 --> 00:16:22.305
Some are larger;
some are smaller.

00:16:22.305 --> 00:16:24.430
One of the things I want
to take a minute to stress

00:16:24.430 --> 00:16:27.970
is that the discount is only
one part of the equation.

00:16:27.970 --> 00:16:30.750
Don't be fooled by
a larger number.

00:16:30.750 --> 00:16:33.000
I'm sure we've all seen
the commercials where

00:16:33.000 --> 00:16:35.430
there's 20% off the sofa.

00:16:35.430 --> 00:16:40.350
But if the sofa was marked
up 4000% in the first place,

00:16:40.350 --> 00:16:42.210
you might be better
off with the guy

00:16:42.210 --> 00:16:45.390
down the street that's
offering a low everyday price.

00:16:45.390 --> 00:16:47.310
So it's a little bit like that.

00:16:47.310 --> 00:16:50.910
You need to look at factors
like, what's the list price?

00:16:50.910 --> 00:16:54.030
What kind of flexibility are you
giving up to get the discounts?

00:16:54.030 --> 00:16:56.190
What kind of
strings or attached?

00:16:56.190 --> 00:16:57.690
And are you paying
for hidden things

00:16:57.690 --> 00:17:00.090
that you don't actually need?

00:17:00.090 --> 00:17:01.590
And more often than
not what we find

00:17:01.590 --> 00:17:03.960
is when people do that
whole calculation,

00:17:03.960 --> 00:17:07.050
Google comes out ahead in terms
of what you save by default,

00:17:07.050 --> 00:17:09.810
and what you can save by
making optimizations to usage.

00:17:13.300 --> 00:17:17.440
So in 2014, when we launched
Google Cloud Platform,

00:17:17.440 --> 00:17:20.710
we introduced a new way to
bring discounts to customers.

00:17:20.710 --> 00:17:22.720
And we think it's a better way.

00:17:22.720 --> 00:17:25.420
So when you use our
VMs consistently,

00:17:25.420 --> 00:17:27.800
it's more efficient for us.

00:17:27.800 --> 00:17:30.160
And we think our
principle of fairness

00:17:30.160 --> 00:17:32.650
means that when we save
money because you're

00:17:32.650 --> 00:17:35.380
using our cloud in a way
that's more efficient for us,

00:17:35.380 --> 00:17:38.090
you deserve to get that back.

00:17:38.090 --> 00:17:40.502
So that's what's Sustained
Use Discounts to you.

00:17:40.502 --> 00:17:42.460
And the nice thing about
that is that you still

00:17:42.460 --> 00:17:46.480
maintain all the flexibility
of an on demand cloud.

00:17:46.480 --> 00:17:49.330
And you can start and stop your
instances whenever you want.

00:17:49.330 --> 00:17:51.730
You can change
your usage pattern.

00:17:51.730 --> 00:17:52.692
It's all up to you.

00:17:52.692 --> 00:17:54.400
And we'll just give
you the best discount

00:17:54.400 --> 00:17:57.000
that your usage qualifies
for automatically

00:17:57.000 --> 00:17:59.370
on a month by month basis.

00:17:59.370 --> 00:18:01.500
So one detail I
want to mention here

00:18:01.500 --> 00:18:03.660
is that there's actually
two modes of calculation

00:18:03.660 --> 00:18:05.490
for Sustained Use Discount.

00:18:05.490 --> 00:18:09.240
If you want, you can call
them SUD 1.0 and SUD 2.0.

00:18:09.240 --> 00:18:12.450
The first this is the original
Sustained Used Discount

00:18:12.450 --> 00:18:15.360
that we launched, which
uses a concept called

00:18:15.360 --> 00:18:16.530
inferred instances.

00:18:16.530 --> 00:18:19.110
And I want to talk about what
that is in just a minute.

00:18:19.110 --> 00:18:22.330
The second is what we do
with custom machine types.

00:18:22.330 --> 00:18:24.840
So when we launched
custom machine types,

00:18:24.840 --> 00:18:27.120
the concept of
inferred instances

00:18:27.120 --> 00:18:29.820
got a little complicated because
with custom machine types

00:18:29.820 --> 00:18:32.370
you can essentially have
thousands of different shapes

00:18:32.370 --> 00:18:33.510
of machines.

00:18:33.510 --> 00:18:35.400
And so what inferred
instances does

00:18:35.400 --> 00:18:38.286
is it combines together
usage of same shape.

00:18:38.286 --> 00:18:39.660
But now with custom
machine types

00:18:39.660 --> 00:18:41.250
you won't have same shapes.

00:18:41.250 --> 00:18:45.600
So what we do instead is we
look at the total chunk of CPUs

00:18:45.600 --> 00:18:47.520
and RAM that you use
throughout the month,

00:18:47.520 --> 00:18:52.420
and we give you a similar
discount for sustained usage.

00:18:52.420 --> 00:18:56.175
So just briefly, what
is an inferred instance?

00:18:56.175 --> 00:18:58.300
This is something we
sometimes get questions about,

00:18:58.300 --> 00:19:00.580
so I wanted to explain
a little bit more.

00:19:00.580 --> 00:19:08.320
So in this graphic, imagine that
you're using some 1 core VMs,

00:19:08.320 --> 00:19:09.940
and you use five
different instances

00:19:09.940 --> 00:19:11.620
at various times
in the month as is

00:19:11.620 --> 00:19:15.410
represented on the left
side in the left crash.

00:19:15.410 --> 00:19:18.220
So you use instance 1
for about half the month.

00:19:18.220 --> 00:19:20.830
Sometime about the tenth of
the month you start instance 2.

00:19:20.830 --> 00:19:22.496
And then about halfway
through the month

00:19:22.496 --> 00:19:24.970
you start 3, and 4 and 5.

00:19:24.970 --> 00:19:27.529
So on the surface of
it if you understand

00:19:27.529 --> 00:19:30.070
that Sustained Use Discounts--
the whole point is we give you

00:19:30.070 --> 00:19:33.400
more discount for the more you
use it throughout the month.

00:19:33.400 --> 00:19:34.900
It doesn't necessarily
look like you

00:19:34.900 --> 00:19:36.130
would get a whole
ton of discount

00:19:36.130 --> 00:19:38.560
because none of those instances
are being used for very

00:19:38.560 --> 00:19:39.880
much in the month at all.

00:19:39.880 --> 00:19:42.760
They would get the first
tier for 25% usage,

00:19:42.760 --> 00:19:44.980
but maybe not much more.

00:19:44.980 --> 00:19:48.700
So we didn't think that was
necessarily fair because it

00:19:48.700 --> 00:19:51.280
doesn't matter much
to our efficiency

00:19:51.280 --> 00:19:54.940
if you run one instance
here, and then shut it down

00:19:54.940 --> 00:19:56.900
for a while, and then
run another instance

00:19:56.900 --> 00:19:58.930
the next day that's
exactly the same shape,

00:19:58.930 --> 00:20:00.680
and run that for the
rest of the month.

00:20:00.680 --> 00:20:04.100
So what we do is we
re-imagine your usage pattern.

00:20:04.100 --> 00:20:07.690
We reinterpret it to look
like the grass in the middle.

00:20:07.690 --> 00:20:11.080
So you can see we flip
the number 3 VM down.

00:20:11.080 --> 00:20:12.310
And this is just conceptual.

00:20:12.310 --> 00:20:15.160
This is a big data
processing problem

00:20:15.160 --> 00:20:18.130
that our billing system
does automatically for you.

00:20:18.130 --> 00:20:22.060
But conceptually,
we've taken VM number 3

00:20:22.060 --> 00:20:25.810
and flipped it down as
if it were part of VM 1.

00:20:25.810 --> 00:20:29.385
And then we've aligned
VMs 2 and 4 and part of 5

00:20:29.385 --> 00:20:31.960
as if they were a second VM.

00:20:31.960 --> 00:20:35.200
And then we've left the rest
of 5 as if it was a third VM.

00:20:35.200 --> 00:20:38.290
So now this graph
on the right is what

00:20:38.290 --> 00:20:39.624
we compute your discount on.

00:20:39.624 --> 00:20:42.040
And it's a much bigger discount
than you would have gotten

00:20:42.040 --> 00:20:43.600
in the graph in the left.

00:20:43.600 --> 00:20:45.280
So you can see that
it looks like you

00:20:45.280 --> 00:20:49.000
used one inferred instance
for the entire month.

00:20:49.000 --> 00:20:51.737
And then the second instance
you used for over half a month.

00:20:51.737 --> 00:20:54.070
And then the third instance
you used for about a quarter

00:20:54.070 --> 00:20:54.611
of the month.

00:20:54.611 --> 00:20:57.400
So each of those will get
a different tier of SUD.

00:20:57.400 --> 00:20:59.890
But it will be a
much higher tier

00:20:59.890 --> 00:21:02.620
than you would have gotten
in combination on the left.

00:21:02.620 --> 00:21:07.030
So this is one of the
ways that we calculate

00:21:07.030 --> 00:21:08.710
what's the best deal for you.

00:21:12.450 --> 00:21:16.290
So we've had Sustained Used
Discounts in production

00:21:16.290 --> 00:21:18.180
for a few years now.

00:21:18.180 --> 00:21:20.910
And customers really liked them.

00:21:20.910 --> 00:21:23.396
And they're getting a lot
of savings out of them.

00:21:23.396 --> 00:21:25.020
But some of the
conversations we've had

00:21:25.020 --> 00:21:28.470
is, is there any way you can
give me a better discount?

00:21:28.470 --> 00:21:31.620
Sustained Used Discount,
it resets every month.

00:21:31.620 --> 00:21:34.560
But many customers
say to us, hey,

00:21:34.560 --> 00:21:37.530
I know that I'm going to use
a certain amount of resources

00:21:37.530 --> 00:21:40.309
100% of the time
to run my website.

00:21:40.309 --> 00:21:42.100
And that's just getting
bigger every month.

00:21:42.100 --> 00:21:45.330
So is there some way that you
can give me a better discount

00:21:45.330 --> 00:21:47.610
if I tell you how
much I'm going to use

00:21:47.610 --> 00:21:51.920
at a minimum for the whole
year, or maybe longer?

00:21:51.920 --> 00:21:56.440
So that brings us to
the question of product

00:21:56.440 --> 00:21:58.840
we announced this morning
called Committed Use Discounts.

00:22:02.380 --> 00:22:05.104
So basically the premise of
the product is if you knew

00:22:05.104 --> 00:22:06.520
and you could tell
Google that you

00:22:06.520 --> 00:22:08.811
were going to use a certain
minimum amount of resources

00:22:08.811 --> 00:22:11.170
all year, shouldn't you
get a bigger discount?

00:22:14.060 --> 00:22:17.840
And there's one obvious
answer to that-- we say yes.

00:22:17.840 --> 00:22:20.275
So how does it work?

00:22:20.275 --> 00:22:22.650
Basically we give you the best
discounts we possibly can.

00:22:22.650 --> 00:22:26.480
If you agree to buy resources
for one year or for three.

00:22:26.480 --> 00:22:29.060
But don't worry, we don't
require any large upfront

00:22:29.060 --> 00:22:29.596
payments.

00:22:29.596 --> 00:22:31.220
You can pay us monthly
and that's cool.

00:22:31.220 --> 00:22:33.730
We've got $30 billion we're
spending on infrastructure.

00:22:33.730 --> 00:22:37.130
And we don't need to
charge you all up front.

00:22:37.130 --> 00:22:38.840
Your promise is
good enough for us.

00:22:38.840 --> 00:22:42.680
So what we do with that, though,
is give you all the flexibility

00:22:42.680 --> 00:22:44.930
so that you can make
changes whenever you want.

00:22:44.930 --> 00:22:46.730
If you decide one
day that you're

00:22:46.730 --> 00:22:48.380
going to go into
your application code

00:22:48.380 --> 00:22:50.600
and optimize it, and all
of a sudden you find a bug,

00:22:50.600 --> 00:22:54.230
and it turns out that you
need 50% less compute than you

00:22:54.230 --> 00:22:56.340
thought you did, awesome.

00:22:56.340 --> 00:22:57.530
Change the instance sizes.

00:22:57.530 --> 00:22:59.120
Keep on going.

00:22:59.120 --> 00:23:01.460
And you'll still
get your discount.

00:23:01.460 --> 00:23:06.320
Additionally, we don't
charge you any discount

00:23:06.320 --> 00:23:07.790
to get that flexibility.

00:23:07.790 --> 00:23:10.610
So some competing
products, if you

00:23:10.610 --> 00:23:12.470
want to be able to
convert them, you

00:23:12.470 --> 00:23:14.210
will lose some of the discount.

00:23:14.210 --> 00:23:15.710
You'll not get the
maximum discount.

00:23:15.710 --> 00:23:17.750
And that's not how ours works.

00:23:17.750 --> 00:23:19.980
So how much?

00:23:19.980 --> 00:23:24.330
So for predefined machine
types, you'll get 37% off

00:23:24.330 --> 00:23:26.050
for a one year discount--

00:23:26.050 --> 00:23:27.600
for one year commitment.

00:23:27.600 --> 00:23:30.347
And for three years
you'll get 55%.

00:23:30.347 --> 00:23:32.430
And these numbers are
actually a little bit higher

00:23:32.430 --> 00:23:35.490
for a custom machine
type, they're 40 and 57,

00:23:35.490 --> 00:23:38.890
respectively.

00:23:38.890 --> 00:23:41.520
So the way that we
make that flexible

00:23:41.520 --> 00:23:43.470
is that the whole
thing is designed based

00:23:43.470 --> 00:23:45.154
on the underlying resources.

00:23:45.154 --> 00:23:46.570
So if you think
back a minute ago,

00:23:46.570 --> 00:23:49.140
when I was talking about
Sustained Use Discounts,

00:23:49.140 --> 00:23:52.920
and how SUD 2.0 looks at
the number of cores and RAM

00:23:52.920 --> 00:23:56.490
that you've used, Committed
Use Discounts are the same way.

00:23:56.490 --> 00:24:00.180
So rather than trying to predict
how many machines you need

00:24:00.180 --> 00:24:04.770
of a certain size in what
zone, just tell us how much CPU

00:24:04.770 --> 00:24:06.600
and how much RAM,
and then you can

00:24:06.600 --> 00:24:08.495
use those in any combination.

00:24:08.495 --> 00:24:10.620
So it sounds like we're
asking you to tell us more,

00:24:10.620 --> 00:24:12.660
but it's actually simpler
because most customers already

00:24:12.660 --> 00:24:13.410
know that math.

00:24:16.290 --> 00:24:18.450
Additionally then,
like I said, they're

00:24:18.450 --> 00:24:20.700
not tied to any
particular VM shape.

00:24:20.700 --> 00:24:24.880
But they're also not tied to any
particular VM instance itself.

00:24:24.880 --> 00:24:29.190
So what we do is we simply
look at how much was running

00:24:29.190 --> 00:24:31.080
at every moment in time--

00:24:31.080 --> 00:24:35.820
every minute-- and we take
the first chunk of that,

00:24:35.820 --> 00:24:38.280
and we give you a Committed
Use Discount on it.

00:24:38.280 --> 00:24:41.262
But the thing then is,
whatever's above that,

00:24:41.262 --> 00:24:43.470
we still give you permanent
billing and Sustained Use

00:24:43.470 --> 00:24:45.070
Discount on all the rest.

00:24:45.070 --> 00:24:49.075
So there's no penalty to having
a Committed Use Discount as

00:24:49.075 --> 00:24:50.700
long as that is the
minimum that you're

00:24:50.700 --> 00:24:52.541
going to be using
because you're still

00:24:52.541 --> 00:24:54.540
going to get the full
discounts on all the rest.

00:24:54.540 --> 00:24:57.870
So if you're not sure how
much to buy, start small.

00:24:57.870 --> 00:24:59.495
See how your
workload is running.

00:24:59.495 --> 00:25:01.620
And then you can increase
over time if you need to.

00:25:01.620 --> 00:25:03.036
And you can know
that you're still

00:25:03.036 --> 00:25:05.490
getting a really competitive
discount on the rest of it.

00:25:09.859 --> 00:25:11.400
So I'll give us a
little example just

00:25:11.400 --> 00:25:13.350
to show you what
that looks like.

00:25:13.350 --> 00:25:15.206
Very simply in this
picture, if you

00:25:15.206 --> 00:25:16.830
imagine that there
is a threshold where

00:25:16.830 --> 00:25:18.660
you have a certain
amount of committed use,

00:25:18.660 --> 00:25:20.940
and then above that you're
getting sustained use,

00:25:20.940 --> 00:25:24.810
you can see where VM A
is shut down for a while

00:25:24.810 --> 00:25:26.280
and then comes back.

00:25:26.280 --> 00:25:31.570
But what we've done is said, OK,
well, for that period of time,

00:25:31.570 --> 00:25:34.260
the usage from VM E
gets committed use.

00:25:34.260 --> 00:25:36.135
And then later it'll
get sustained use again.

00:25:36.135 --> 00:25:38.301
And obviously this is going
to look like a much more

00:25:38.301 --> 00:25:39.520
complex picture in reality.

00:25:43.310 --> 00:25:46.169
So finally, I want to
talk about some tuning--

00:25:46.169 --> 00:25:47.960
some features you can
use to take advantage

00:25:47.960 --> 00:25:51.860
of tuning your workload so that
it's best configured to get

00:25:51.860 --> 00:25:53.230
the maximum savings on GCE.

00:25:57.940 --> 00:25:59.440
So the first feature
is rightsizing.

00:25:59.440 --> 00:26:00.690
And you've probably seen this.

00:26:00.690 --> 00:26:04.350
We talked about it in a
couple of different places.

00:26:04.350 --> 00:26:08.210
The social media had
a kind of common idea

00:26:08.210 --> 00:26:10.100
when we introduced
this features.

00:26:10.100 --> 00:26:11.600
What are they, crazy?

00:26:11.600 --> 00:26:15.860
They're telling me how to
spend less money on purpose?

00:26:15.860 --> 00:26:17.090
Yes, we are.

00:26:17.090 --> 00:26:21.490
So we would rather that you
optimize your infrastructure

00:26:21.490 --> 00:26:23.777
usage for what your
workload actually needs,

00:26:23.777 --> 00:26:25.610
rather than trying to
optimize your workload

00:26:25.610 --> 00:26:28.820
to fit in particular sized
infrastructure of ours.

00:26:28.820 --> 00:26:32.450
And we would rather that you
buy what's valuable to you

00:26:32.450 --> 00:26:33.740
and nothing more.

00:26:33.740 --> 00:26:37.370
We'll happily sell the rest
of that to somebody else.

00:26:37.370 --> 00:26:39.890
So what this does
is automatically

00:26:39.890 --> 00:26:44.270
monitors your CPU and RAM
usage from outside the VM.

00:26:44.270 --> 00:26:47.390
And if it sees that you never
use more than a certain amount

00:26:47.390 --> 00:26:50.640
of CPU, we'll recommend
that you scale down the CPU.

00:26:50.640 --> 00:26:53.160
If you're never using more
than a certain amount of RAM,

00:26:53.160 --> 00:26:55.080
it'll scale that down too.

00:26:55.080 --> 00:26:57.410
And turns out is one
additional detail of this,

00:26:57.410 --> 00:27:00.510
if you actually install this
Stackdriver monitoring agent,

00:27:00.510 --> 00:27:02.220
then we get better
memory information,

00:27:02.220 --> 00:27:04.770
and we can actually make a
better recommendation for you.

00:27:04.770 --> 00:27:06.950
But either way, you'll
get a good recommendation

00:27:06.950 --> 00:27:09.380
on what kind of
machine you want.

00:27:09.380 --> 00:27:11.180
And then, simply, all
you need to do there

00:27:11.180 --> 00:27:12.920
is on the right hand side.

00:27:12.920 --> 00:27:16.070
Click that recommendation,
and you can--

00:27:16.070 --> 00:27:18.410
you need to shut down and
quickly restart your VM

00:27:18.410 --> 00:27:21.140
in order to change the size.

00:27:21.140 --> 00:27:24.410
It's just that simple.

00:27:24.410 --> 00:27:27.320
Finally, I also want to
talk about preemptible VMs.

00:27:27.320 --> 00:27:30.020
So the idea with
preemptible VMs is

00:27:30.020 --> 00:27:33.830
to offer a product that is
as low cost as we can make it

00:27:33.830 --> 00:27:37.620
and optimized for
short term workloads.

00:27:37.620 --> 00:27:42.470
So if you have a large
scale fault tolerant

00:27:42.470 --> 00:27:45.980
workload that can withstand
a few of its workers

00:27:45.980 --> 00:27:50.130
shutting down without losing
too much of your overall work,

00:27:50.130 --> 00:27:53.300
then that's probably a job
that is really ideal for permit

00:27:53.300 --> 00:27:54.740
preemptable VMs

00:27:54.740 --> 00:27:57.620
And if you use those, you'll
find that there are almost 80%

00:27:57.620 --> 00:27:59.480
off of list prices.

00:27:59.480 --> 00:28:00.770
In some cases, they are 80%.

00:28:00.770 --> 00:28:03.226
In some cases, they're
a little bit less.

00:28:03.226 --> 00:28:05.600
The only restrictions are that
there's a maximum lifetime

00:28:05.600 --> 00:28:07.490
on that VM of 24 hours.

00:28:07.490 --> 00:28:09.590
And there's a small
probability chance

00:28:09.590 --> 00:28:11.750
that we might take
back the VM early.

00:28:11.750 --> 00:28:13.940
We'll give you a short
warning of about 30 seconds

00:28:13.940 --> 00:28:17.220
and say, hey, we need that back.

00:28:17.220 --> 00:28:20.090
So a lot of workloads-- and you
can see there's a whole list

00:28:20.090 --> 00:28:21.490
of different ones below--

00:28:21.490 --> 00:28:24.140
are finding that that's
a really efficient way

00:28:24.140 --> 00:28:26.570
to get large amounts of compute.

00:28:26.570 --> 00:28:29.600
So one of my colleagues
in a room down the hall

00:28:29.600 --> 00:28:31.580
right now is talking
about an example

00:28:31.580 --> 00:28:36.410
where Fermilab ran 160,000
cores preemptible in order

00:28:36.410 --> 00:28:39.250
to calculate higher energy
physics experiments.

00:28:39.250 --> 00:28:43.010
And he actually doubled the
compute fleet of Fermi National

00:28:43.010 --> 00:28:46.140
Accelerator Laboratory in
finding exotic new physics

00:28:46.140 --> 00:28:46.640
particles.

00:28:46.640 --> 00:28:48.350
So it's just one
example of what you

00:28:48.350 --> 00:28:52.010
can do with preemptible VMs
in a very affordable way.

00:28:52.010 --> 00:28:56.540
But there's lots of other use
cases from genomics and genome

00:28:56.540 --> 00:28:59.930
sequencing to computational
chemistry fluid dynamics etc.

00:29:02.867 --> 00:29:04.700
So finally, I want to
talk a little bit more

00:29:04.700 --> 00:29:05.730
about our autoscaler.

00:29:05.730 --> 00:29:08.570
I showed the graph earlier
that talked about how

00:29:08.570 --> 00:29:10.130
permanent billing helps.

00:29:10.130 --> 00:29:12.800
But there's actually some other
features of our autoscaler

00:29:12.800 --> 00:29:15.110
that help save money too.

00:29:15.110 --> 00:29:18.020
So first of all, the
fact that it's extremely

00:29:18.020 --> 00:29:20.960
fast and reactive to workloads.

00:29:20.960 --> 00:29:23.120
So unlike some
other autoscalers,

00:29:23.120 --> 00:29:26.330
we've actually never
had to consider

00:29:26.330 --> 00:29:29.300
building a feature where
you would either schedule

00:29:29.300 --> 00:29:34.440
in advance for it to
scale up, or pre-warm VMs

00:29:34.440 --> 00:29:36.470
so it could react faster.

00:29:36.470 --> 00:29:40.070
We kind of feel like that's not
what auto is supposed to mean.

00:29:40.070 --> 00:29:42.530
So ours just scales.

00:29:42.530 --> 00:29:45.980
And because our VMs can do
it in 30 seconds or less,

00:29:45.980 --> 00:29:48.200
we can actually react to
spikes very quickly, up

00:29:48.200 --> 00:29:49.910
to millions of QPIs.

00:29:49.910 --> 00:29:51.980
And what that means
is you don't have

00:29:51.980 --> 00:29:55.250
to have VM sitting around
waiting or running longer

00:29:55.250 --> 00:29:56.780
in case the spike comes back.

00:29:56.780 --> 00:30:00.164
You don't have to have
them running just in case

00:30:00.164 --> 00:30:01.580
there is a spike
because you never

00:30:01.580 --> 00:30:03.580
know what might happen
in social media.

00:30:03.580 --> 00:30:05.330
You don't have to worry
about any of that.

00:30:05.330 --> 00:30:07.100
And all of those
VMs that might be

00:30:07.100 --> 00:30:09.350
running handle
scenarios like that

00:30:09.350 --> 00:30:10.970
are just money you're wasting.

00:30:10.970 --> 00:30:13.790
So just the fact
that our autoscaler

00:30:13.790 --> 00:30:17.960
works like this actually
saves you money as well.

00:30:17.960 --> 00:30:20.480
And you might even
actually consider

00:30:20.480 --> 00:30:24.410
whether workloads you might
not think are web server.

00:30:24.410 --> 00:30:26.660
For example, those
workloads might

00:30:26.660 --> 00:30:29.930
benefit from a fast autoscaler
and help you automatically

00:30:29.930 --> 00:30:33.107
tune what you're using.

00:30:33.107 --> 00:30:35.690
So that's a number of different
pricing features and discounts

00:30:35.690 --> 00:30:37.430
that are available on GCE.

00:30:37.430 --> 00:30:41.180
And now I'd like to invite
Michael Crandell, the CEO

00:30:41.180 --> 00:30:44.030
and founder of RightScale up
to show you what some of that

00:30:44.030 --> 00:30:45.540
looks like in practice.

00:30:45.540 --> 00:30:46.060
Michael.

