00:31:37.390 --> 00:31:40.730
A little background about us.

00:31:40.730 --> 00:31:43.550
At Rightscale we focus
on cloud management.

00:31:43.550 --> 00:31:45.740
And ever since about
five years ago we

00:31:45.740 --> 00:31:49.940
acquired a small startup that
was focusing on a piece called

00:31:49.940 --> 00:31:53.300
Cloud Cost Management,
Cloud Cost Analytics,

00:31:53.300 --> 00:31:58.130
we've seen increasing interest
in both the opportunity

00:31:58.130 --> 00:32:01.810
that can be achieved through
savings and the challenge.

00:32:01.810 --> 00:32:05.630
And so let's dive right
in on the challenge side.

00:32:05.630 --> 00:32:08.630
We also run every year a
State Of The Cloud survey.

00:32:08.630 --> 00:32:11.090
We get about 1,000
responses from companies

00:32:11.090 --> 00:32:15.100
ranging from small businesses
to enterprise level companies.

00:32:15.100 --> 00:32:17.600
And we ask a bunch of questions.

00:32:17.600 --> 00:32:20.120
And we ask one about waste.

00:32:20.120 --> 00:32:23.810
What do you think you're
wasting in your cloud spend?

00:32:23.810 --> 00:32:27.190
I think Hertzel shared that
this morning, this stat.

00:32:27.190 --> 00:32:30.030
But the self-reported
waste, in other words,

00:32:30.030 --> 00:32:33.380
the waste that people think
that's occurring is about 30%.

00:32:33.380 --> 00:32:36.350
We've found from our product
and in close engagements

00:32:36.350 --> 00:32:42.440
with customers that often is
upwards of 40, up toward 45%.

00:32:42.440 --> 00:32:44.690
So it's pretty clear--
everyone agrees--

00:32:44.690 --> 00:32:46.760
there is a lot of
waste, and that also

00:32:46.760 --> 00:32:49.070
means there's a
lot of opportunity.

00:32:49.070 --> 00:32:51.080
So I wanted to
share with you today

00:32:51.080 --> 00:32:54.020
from Rightscale's perspective
in the field three

00:32:54.020 --> 00:32:59.390
of the biggest areas that we
see that are waste out there,

00:32:59.390 --> 00:33:01.520
and that are also
opportunities for savings.

00:33:01.520 --> 00:33:03.330
And they focus around
these three areas.

00:33:03.330 --> 00:33:07.820
One is not taking advantage of
discounts, discount structures

00:33:07.820 --> 00:33:08.680
that are available.

00:33:08.680 --> 00:33:11.480
The second is running
workloads that

00:33:11.480 --> 00:33:15.950
don't need to run all the time,
and still running them 24/7.

00:33:15.950 --> 00:33:19.460
And the last area is
over provisioning, right?

00:33:19.460 --> 00:33:23.780
Underutilized instances and
VMs that are running out there.

00:33:23.780 --> 00:33:26.480
And so we'd like to focus
across these three areas

00:33:26.480 --> 00:33:29.027
on three of the
feature offerings

00:33:29.027 --> 00:33:30.860
that you've heard about
today-- some of them

00:33:30.860 --> 00:33:34.310
brand new offerings, committed
use and sustained use

00:33:34.310 --> 00:33:39.240
discounts, preemptibles,
and custom machine types.

00:33:39.240 --> 00:33:42.740
So let's launch in,
first on the first use

00:33:42.740 --> 00:33:45.290
case, which is
optimizing and taking

00:33:45.290 --> 00:33:48.350
full advantage of discounts.

00:33:48.350 --> 00:33:51.770
Starting point there, most
of us are familiar with

00:33:51.770 --> 00:33:55.880
what's probably the oldest and
most familiar discount scheme

00:33:55.880 --> 00:33:58.370
out there-- it's from
Amazon Web Services--

00:33:58.370 --> 00:34:00.500
called reserved instances.

00:34:00.500 --> 00:34:03.530
And many of you may be familiar,
but just a quick refresher,

00:34:03.530 --> 00:34:05.270
there are many types of these.

00:34:05.270 --> 00:34:08.130
But to start with standard
reserved instances,

00:34:08.130 --> 00:34:11.179
you're basically, in
order to get a discount,

00:34:11.179 --> 00:34:14.840
you pick a specific size of
instance in a specific instance

00:34:14.840 --> 00:34:17.900
family in a particular
region, and commit

00:34:17.900 --> 00:34:22.850
to either a year or three
years to gain that discount.

00:34:22.850 --> 00:34:28.340
And they also have a family
of RIs called convertible RIs.

00:34:28.340 --> 00:34:31.130
And it's important to note
the discount is lower there,

00:34:31.130 --> 00:34:34.980
and it's only available with
a three year commitment.

00:34:34.980 --> 00:34:38.120
So what we find among
customers in this RI world

00:34:38.120 --> 00:34:39.790
is actually a lot of resistance.

00:34:39.790 --> 00:34:41.570
People struggle with this.

00:34:41.570 --> 00:34:44.150
And the reason they struggle
with it is pretty simple.

00:34:44.150 --> 00:34:47.760
It's because they're being
asked to predict the future.

00:34:47.760 --> 00:34:50.300
And quite often the workloads
that they're dealing with,

00:34:50.300 --> 00:34:52.580
the apps that
they're dealing with,

00:34:52.580 --> 00:34:56.389
and the approaches they're
taking to development test

00:34:56.389 --> 00:35:00.260
and production in the cloud
aren't that predictable.

00:35:00.260 --> 00:35:03.750
And so you see some of
the reasons listed here.

00:35:03.750 --> 00:35:06.080
I'm not sure if I might
change architectures.

00:35:06.080 --> 00:35:10.220
We're not sure what might happen
to the utilization of our app.

00:35:10.220 --> 00:35:12.200
There may be new
types of instances

00:35:12.200 --> 00:35:14.300
coming with bigger discounts.

00:35:14.300 --> 00:35:17.420
So there's fear of making a
commitment that you're not

00:35:17.420 --> 00:35:18.520
going to fully use.

00:35:18.520 --> 00:35:21.080
And there there's a
lack of flexibility

00:35:21.080 --> 00:35:24.380
that causes hesitation.

00:35:24.380 --> 00:35:27.890
So the reality is,
really, that in many apps

00:35:27.890 --> 00:35:29.810
and many, many workloads
you're actually

00:35:29.810 --> 00:35:31.280
dealing with constant change.

00:35:31.280 --> 00:35:35.450
The approach to development and
test et cetera, is evolving.

00:35:35.450 --> 00:35:37.640
Your architecture is
changing and evolving.

00:35:37.640 --> 00:35:39.810
You're not sure what
your needs will be.

00:35:39.810 --> 00:35:42.740
And we've often asked
ourselves, wouldn't it

00:35:42.740 --> 00:35:45.920
be great if we could
get the discount,

00:35:45.920 --> 00:35:49.100
but not be locked into
the specific instance

00:35:49.100 --> 00:35:51.200
size commitments?

00:35:51.200 --> 00:35:52.880
And here's an example of that.

00:35:52.880 --> 00:35:55.370
It's just a hypothetical
example of--

00:35:55.370 --> 00:35:58.790
I think it's five
instances on the left.

00:35:58.790 --> 00:36:01.020
Maybe in the future
what you're using now

00:36:01.020 --> 00:36:04.640
needs to change to
that 8 instance model

00:36:04.640 --> 00:36:07.220
where, in this case, you're
using smaller instances

00:36:07.220 --> 00:36:09.920
and distributing
the workload more.

00:36:09.920 --> 00:36:11.720
Wouldn't it be great
if you could get that?

00:36:11.720 --> 00:36:14.300
Well, actually, this is
just another description

00:36:14.300 --> 00:36:17.480
of what's been announced
this week with Committed Use

00:36:17.480 --> 00:36:19.070
Discount.

00:36:19.070 --> 00:36:24.110
And again, you can get some
of that with convertible RIs,

00:36:24.110 --> 00:36:27.410
but you're committed to three
years, which in our experience

00:36:27.410 --> 00:36:32.140
is really quite a long time
frame for most customers.

00:36:32.140 --> 00:36:34.030
Let's take a look at
a specific example.

00:36:34.030 --> 00:36:38.370
This is an anonymised real
customer usage with cost

00:36:38.370 --> 00:36:41.550
on the vertical and, over
time, on the horizontal.

00:36:41.550 --> 00:36:45.930
And you can see that there is a
pretty steady base usage there

00:36:45.930 --> 00:36:47.220
that's growing slightly.

00:36:47.220 --> 00:36:50.610
And then during weekdays
some transient usage.

00:36:50.610 --> 00:36:53.770
That's dev in test
in this example.

00:36:53.770 --> 00:36:57.870
So if we map this to both
providers comparing AWS

00:36:57.870 --> 00:37:01.680
and Google, on the AWS side
what we would recommend--

00:37:01.680 --> 00:37:05.010
and we happen to know this
customer is using about 80%

00:37:05.010 --> 00:37:06.540
of those instances--

00:37:06.540 --> 00:37:11.400
are production, and about
50% for dev in test.

00:37:11.400 --> 00:37:15.240
We would recommend if they're
having to lock in and commit

00:37:15.240 --> 00:37:17.880
to standard RIs, we'd
say, why don't you

00:37:17.880 --> 00:37:20.670
limit your exposure
there to 65% in order

00:37:20.670 --> 00:37:24.090
to gain savings, but leave
yourself some flexibility

00:37:24.090 --> 00:37:25.260
for change?

00:37:25.260 --> 00:37:28.620
If you're going to commit
to convertible RIs,

00:37:28.620 --> 00:37:30.490
go ahead and go up to 90%.

00:37:30.490 --> 00:37:33.090
So that's what our
recommendation would typically

00:37:33.090 --> 00:37:38.200
be over a long experience
helping customers save money.

00:37:38.200 --> 00:37:40.090
Take a look at that on Google.

00:37:40.090 --> 00:37:41.650
With Google pricing,
it's pretty much

00:37:41.650 --> 00:37:44.710
a no brainer with the new
Committed Use Discount.

00:37:44.710 --> 00:37:47.110
Just go ahead and
take 90% of it.

00:37:47.110 --> 00:37:50.260
And buy into that with
a committed use discount

00:37:50.260 --> 00:37:52.810
because there's no
flexibility trade-off.

00:37:52.810 --> 00:37:55.330
And let the Sustained
Use Discount

00:37:55.330 --> 00:37:58.740
with the inferred
instance capability

00:37:58.740 --> 00:38:01.210
that Paul mentioned earlier
take care of the rest.

00:38:01.210 --> 00:38:04.510
That's going to automatically
fit the pieces together

00:38:04.510 --> 00:38:09.040
of the transient users into
the most optimal cost savings

00:38:09.040 --> 00:38:11.860
for you.

00:38:11.860 --> 00:38:16.510
If we map that on to actual
dollars, what you see here--

00:38:16.510 --> 00:38:17.780
we've done the calcs for you.

00:38:17.780 --> 00:38:20.560
I'm not going to go
through every detail there.

00:38:20.560 --> 00:38:25.150
But we did map out
three different levels

00:38:25.150 --> 00:38:29.200
with the assumptions that I
mentioned about 50% and 80%

00:38:29.200 --> 00:38:30.370
averaging out.

00:38:30.370 --> 00:38:35.512
So a standard RI, one year
reserved instance approach.

00:38:35.512 --> 00:38:36.970
On the right hand
side, you can see

00:38:36.970 --> 00:38:41.680
you're getting 21% savings
by using that mechanism.

00:38:41.680 --> 00:38:44.980
Three year standard RI, 39%.

00:38:44.980 --> 00:38:49.972
And the more comparable three
year convertible RI is 34%.

00:38:49.972 --> 00:38:51.430
So what we're going
to look at here

00:38:51.430 --> 00:38:54.400
to keep it apples to
apples and try and match

00:38:54.400 --> 00:39:00.100
some of the flexibility in
the Google pricing scheme

00:39:00.100 --> 00:39:03.590
that allows you to shift
instance sizes and families,

00:39:03.590 --> 00:39:06.610
and so on, we're going to
map the first one, which

00:39:06.610 --> 00:39:09.370
is that one year, and
the third one, which

00:39:09.370 --> 00:39:11.080
is a three year convertible.

00:39:11.080 --> 00:39:13.750
Here's what that
looks like on Google.

00:39:13.750 --> 00:39:19.330
35% on the one year, which
represents 28% better

00:39:19.330 --> 00:39:22.730
savings than the AWS
one year standard RI

00:39:22.730 --> 00:39:25.120
approach in this example.

00:39:25.120 --> 00:39:29.710
And then on three
year, 52% savings,

00:39:29.710 --> 00:39:35.560
which represents a 35%
improvement over AWS three year

00:39:35.560 --> 00:39:37.290
convertibles.

00:39:37.290 --> 00:39:40.060
If you summarize all of
that into a single chart,

00:39:40.060 --> 00:39:41.310
here's what you're looking at.

00:39:41.310 --> 00:39:45.090
On one year, you
have better pricing.

00:39:45.090 --> 00:39:46.890
On three year, you
have better pricing

00:39:46.890 --> 00:39:50.970
than both the full three
year without convertible

00:39:50.970 --> 00:39:53.040
and a three year
with convertible.

00:39:53.040 --> 00:39:55.680
And other important
points to remember

00:39:55.680 --> 00:39:58.440
is that on the one
year you still--

00:39:58.440 --> 00:40:01.140
with committed use you
still get flexibility.

00:40:01.140 --> 00:40:03.660
So that's an advantage
on flexibility,

00:40:03.660 --> 00:40:06.330
whether it's one
year or three year.

00:40:06.330 --> 00:40:07.932
You also get more ease of use.

00:40:07.932 --> 00:40:09.390
Because it is
important to remember

00:40:09.390 --> 00:40:13.020
with the convertible
RIs on AWS, you still

00:40:13.020 --> 00:40:17.370
need to track your usage,
and make a special request

00:40:17.370 --> 00:40:21.540
to AWS to sign in instance
to the convertible RI you've

00:40:21.540 --> 00:40:22.410
bought.

00:40:22.410 --> 00:40:26.194
In the Google scheme, that
happens automatically.

00:40:26.194 --> 00:40:28.110
And then, of course, the
final and Third Point

00:40:28.110 --> 00:40:31.210
is it's just less expensive.

00:40:31.210 --> 00:40:33.720
So we think that's pretty
groundbreaking and pretty

00:40:33.720 --> 00:40:35.970
compelling.

00:40:35.970 --> 00:40:39.570
Second use case was
dev in test workloads.

00:40:39.570 --> 00:40:41.460
So this is a starting point.

00:40:41.460 --> 00:40:43.170
We are talking
about savings here.

00:40:43.170 --> 00:40:45.870
And this doesn't really apply
to one cloud or another.

00:40:45.870 --> 00:40:48.810
This applies to any
cloud that you run on.

00:40:48.810 --> 00:40:50.730
And actually, I'll
admit might be

00:40:50.730 --> 00:40:53.830
obvious to you-- it was a little
bit of a eye opener for me

00:40:53.830 --> 00:40:56.280
years ago when I first
looked at it-- if you limit

00:40:56.280 --> 00:41:01.890
your usage on your workload
to only say, 12 hours a day,

00:41:01.890 --> 00:41:06.570
five days a week you're
actually saving 65%.

00:41:06.570 --> 00:41:09.930
And that's a key thing that we
help people with automation.

00:41:09.930 --> 00:41:12.270
And the rest of the
right scale platform

00:41:12.270 --> 00:41:17.250
is to schedule run times
that don't run 24/7

00:41:17.250 --> 00:41:18.620
when they don't need to.

00:41:18.620 --> 00:41:20.370
Paul mentioned a whole
bunch of workloads.

00:41:20.370 --> 00:41:22.530
I think one of the most
common is dev in test

00:41:22.530 --> 00:41:25.480
that we're talking about here.

00:41:25.480 --> 00:41:28.680
So when we map that to
the preemptible model

00:41:28.680 --> 00:41:32.220
that Paul talked about,
we've got four levels there.

00:41:32.220 --> 00:41:35.460
If you run with sustained
only, you see the first price,

00:41:35.460 --> 00:41:37.650
around 67,000.

00:41:37.650 --> 00:41:40.470
If you commit to
a portion of that,

00:41:40.470 --> 00:41:43.470
you can achieve a 9% savings
with the Committed Use

00:41:43.470 --> 00:41:45.360
Discount.

00:41:45.360 --> 00:41:48.630
If you start mixing
committed with preempibles,

00:41:48.630 --> 00:41:53.190
and if you can have a
dev in test workflow

00:41:53.190 --> 00:41:58.140
that is tolerant of
instances being taken away--

00:41:58.140 --> 00:42:00.600
and maybe it's just the loss
of a little time for you

00:42:00.600 --> 00:42:05.490
versus loss of any data
if your dev task flow

00:42:05.490 --> 00:42:07.380
allows for that, which means--

00:42:07.380 --> 00:42:08.100
think about that.

00:42:08.100 --> 00:42:09.420
That's a good thing.

00:42:09.420 --> 00:42:13.410
You could save 40% by
splitting half and half.

00:42:13.410 --> 00:42:16.320
And if you're able to put
all of that dev in test

00:42:16.320 --> 00:42:20.050
on to preemptibles,
it's a 70% savings.

00:42:20.050 --> 00:42:25.800
So there is an analog here with
Amazon's spot instance market,

00:42:25.800 --> 00:42:28.440
difference being that in
that market it is a market

00:42:28.440 --> 00:42:29.940
and you need to bid.

00:42:29.940 --> 00:42:32.760
And so you're not
guaranteed a price.

00:42:32.760 --> 00:42:34.350
You can get pretty
great savings.

00:42:34.350 --> 00:42:36.110
But here you're
guaranteed a price.

00:42:36.110 --> 00:42:39.340
You know what you'll be
paying in this model.

00:42:39.340 --> 00:42:41.230
The last use case
I wanted to share

00:42:41.230 --> 00:42:47.180
was around rightsizing
using custom machine types.

00:42:47.180 --> 00:42:51.260
This is a screen shot of a
part of right scale on cost

00:42:51.260 --> 00:42:54.620
analytics that we call
the utilization report.

00:42:54.620 --> 00:42:57.710
And what you see here
along the horizontal axis

00:42:57.710 --> 00:43:01.700
is how much memory utilization
there is on an instance.

00:43:01.700 --> 00:43:04.160
So farther to the
right, more memory.

00:43:04.160 --> 00:43:08.750
Farther up vertically on the
graph, more CPU utilization.

00:43:08.750 --> 00:43:11.660
The instances or VMs are
represented by the circles.

00:43:11.660 --> 00:43:14.300
And the size of the
circle is the cost

00:43:14.300 --> 00:43:17.000
that you're paying for that VM.

00:43:17.000 --> 00:43:18.920
So immediately
with a single blink

00:43:18.920 --> 00:43:21.560
your eye can go to
the lower left and go,

00:43:21.560 --> 00:43:24.410
I'm paying for stuff
that I'm not using there.

00:43:24.410 --> 00:43:27.470
And that's pretty great
to be able to see.

00:43:27.470 --> 00:43:29.760
Now, sometimes
that's on purpose.

00:43:29.760 --> 00:43:32.990
For example, you might have
a warm spare, hot spare

00:43:32.990 --> 00:43:37.820
sitting there for some sort
of disaster recovery scenario.

00:43:37.820 --> 00:43:38.930
So it might be on purpose.

00:43:38.930 --> 00:43:41.060
But in many cases it's not.

00:43:41.060 --> 00:43:43.550
And when we work
with customers we

00:43:43.550 --> 00:43:45.830
say in that very
lower left, that's

00:43:45.830 --> 00:43:49.250
a candidate for
downsizing a t-shirt size

00:43:49.250 --> 00:43:54.560
or two with the standard
predefined VMs types.

00:43:54.560 --> 00:43:58.160
But more importantly, when we're
talking about custom machine

00:43:58.160 --> 00:44:02.600
types in the upper left,
that's an example of VMs

00:44:02.600 --> 00:44:06.410
where the CPU utilization
is pretty reasonably high,

00:44:06.410 --> 00:44:07.640
but not the memory.

00:44:07.640 --> 00:44:10.940
So you might want to dial in
a custom machine type that

00:44:10.940 --> 00:44:12.740
lowered the memory requirement.

00:44:12.740 --> 00:44:16.250
Same applies in the lower right,
just the converse-- high memory

00:44:16.250 --> 00:44:18.890
utilization, low CPU.

00:44:18.890 --> 00:44:20.970
So here's another example.

00:44:20.970 --> 00:44:26.080
Let's say you need it four CPUs
and 20 gigs in a machine size.

00:44:26.080 --> 00:44:30.230
Standard predefined VMs
on both AWS and Google,

00:44:30.230 --> 00:44:33.140
you'd have to get an
eight CPU and 30 gigabyte.

00:44:33.140 --> 00:44:35.360
And you can see what
the pricing is there.

00:44:35.360 --> 00:44:36.960
And I think that
Google pricing is

00:44:36.960 --> 00:44:39.410
updated for the
announcements this week.

00:44:39.410 --> 00:44:44.460
But if you're able to dial in
a custom VM in this example,

00:44:44.460 --> 00:44:46.070
you save even more.

00:44:46.070 --> 00:44:50.390
You're able to save 42% over
the standard predefined size

00:44:50.390 --> 00:44:54.590
on Google, and 64% over the
standard predefined size

00:44:54.590 --> 00:44:57.050
on AWS.

00:44:57.050 --> 00:45:02.570
So that's a quick tour of these
three approaches to savings.

00:45:02.570 --> 00:45:06.410
And I think the bottom line
is that with the announcements

00:45:06.410 --> 00:45:09.890
you've heard this week, as well
as other mechanisms Google's

00:45:09.890 --> 00:45:12.650
had in place for
pricing, they've

00:45:12.650 --> 00:45:17.060
pretty much upped the ante and
taken the lead in the cloud

00:45:17.060 --> 00:45:21.620
provider space for offering
very customer friendly pricing.

00:45:21.620 --> 00:45:25.340
So I would just say compliments
to the whole Google Cloud team.

00:45:25.340 --> 00:45:26.690
We're very impressed with that.

00:45:26.690 --> 00:45:30.140
And thank you for making all of
this a whole lot easier for us

00:45:30.140 --> 00:45:32.659
customers out there.

00:45:32.659 --> 00:45:34.950
By the way, if you're interested
in learning more about

00:45:34.950 --> 00:45:37.980
Rightscale-- and Paul, why don't
you start coming back up here

00:45:37.980 --> 00:45:40.020
too because we'll do some Q&amp;A--

00:45:40.020 --> 00:45:42.120
we do have a booth
here downstairs.

00:45:42.120 --> 00:45:45.150
We also are doing a webinar,
I think, the two of us

00:45:45.150 --> 00:45:49.290
together later this month with
a deeper dive on March 23rd.

00:45:49.290 --> 00:45:51.120
You can also get that
State Of The Cloud

00:45:51.120 --> 00:45:55.940
report that I referred to
earlier off of our website.

